+{"id": "REPORT-1", "title": "P1: the hourly report mislabels the actor — it calls the LLM's decisions 'JEV' and invents a ladder blocker", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 1, "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "Found by reading my own report against the run it describes. The 20:46 UTC hourly said 'JEV handled every cycle unaided' and 'JEV is deciding unaided' about run long_0927_llm2 — which is the PURE-LLM arm, where JEV is never called (decision_mode stamps 'llm', family 'system2', decision_mode_family 'system2', and escalated is False on every decision row). ROOT CAUSE, in ~/.hermes/scripts/aipp_hourly_facts.py: the script never reads decision_mode at all (0 hits) and its fields are JEV-shaped — participation_ratio is computed from 'jev_answered' (line 103) and missing classes are labelled 'top_jev_missing_classes' (line 109); 'jev_answered' is also counted up (lines 96, 141). In llm mode jev_answered is 0, so the script cannot distinguish 'System Two decided' from 'System One decided' and defaults to naming JEV. SECOND DEFECT, same report: it renders the ladder as a REQUIRED sequence — 'Oak's Lab — NOT proven; goal_achieved still False because the ladder requires it'. The ladder in long_run.py:56 is a DISPLAY list; Oak's Lab is not required, and this run was seeded at Pallet Town so it could never visit the Lab. The report invented a blocker that did not exist. WHY P1: this is the surface the owner reads, and it was obscuring the exact question he was asking (which arm is doing the playing). A status surface that names the wrong actor is worse than one that says nothing. Done-when: the report reads decision_mode/decision_mode_family from the run rows and names the actual decider; ladder rows are marked proven/not-visited/not-applicable relative to the run's seed state instead of required-in-order; and a test asserts an llm-mode run is never described as JEV. Evidence attached for both defects.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T21:06:10.988705+00:00"}
+{"id": "ARM-1", "title": "P1 OWNER DECISION: the shipped default arm (jev) is the arm that cannot leave Pallet Town", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 1, "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "DEFAULT_DECISION_MODE = 'jev' (cron_runner.py:115) — the hybrid, where System One decides and the LLM only repairs on escalation. Measured today and yesterday, same boot state: pure-LLM/controller 6/6 episodes to Route 1 (median 13.5 cycles) vs jev 0/5 (all censored at the 30-cycle cap); the 480-episode full run reached Route 1 once, around episode 202, and only because OR_JEV was 403-ing and the loop fell through to the controller — and never reached Viridian. Today the pure-LLM arm reached VIRIDIAN CITY (verifiable: RAM-read map name at ep021 cycle 2 of long_0927_llm) in 50 minutes. So the default we ship and point at as 'the result' is the arm that stalls. THIS ROW IS A DECISION, NOT A TASK: options are (a) flip the default to llm/system2 and treat the hybrid as the cost-optimisation experiment it was meant to be, (b) keep jev as default but make every benchmark report BOTH arms side by side so the default never again stands in for 'what the system does', or (c) hold until the continuation run (long_0927_llm2) gives a hold-rate for Viridian. Not acting without the owner's call.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T21:06:10.988705+00:00"}
+{"id": "BOOT-1", "title": "P1: the long-run driver's default boot state is a DIFFERENT map position than every measured baseline", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "long_run.py:47 hardcodes BOOT = data/boot.state. Probed with the project's own validator (long_run.probe_state): data/boot.state reads map 'Oak's Lab' (id 40) while data/baselines/base-1_boot.state reads 'Pallet Town' (id 0, md5 81e4ec4e8d1b62002bc78f619c5fc79c) — and Pallet Town is the state CTRL-WIN, DIST-1 and BASE-1 were all measured on. So a run launched the obvious way starts ~a whole map earlier and is silently incomparable to every number in data/baselines/, which is exactly the class of error that makes two runs look different for reasons that are not the agent. Compounding it: long_run.py passes no decision-mode flag to cron_runner.py (it invokes only --run-id, --cycles, --boot-state), so the arm a run used exists only in an ambient env var and is not recoverable from the run's own record. Worked around today by seeding long_run/<RUN_ID>_good.state from the canonical state and setting AIPP_DECISION_MODE explicitly — but that is operator discipline, not a property of the driver. Done-when: the driver takes the boot state and the decision mode as explicit arguments (or env with a loud default) and stamps BOTH into the run's status row and per-episode records, so a run cannot be launched from an unintended state or in an unrecorded arm.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T21:06:10.988705+00:00"}
+{"id": "EFF-1", "title": "P2: autonomy is 1.00 but the agent burned 8 episodes inside Red's House 1F — measure efficiency, not just escalation", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "The pure-LLM run long_0927_llm posted 594 decisions with 0 escalations and 0 errors, and the honest autonomy ratio reads 1.00 — while per-episode final maps show ep001-ep008 ALL ended in 'Red's House 1F', the starting house. Eight episodes (about 19 minutes) to walk out of a two-room building, with every decision counted as an unaided success. The autonomy metric is blind to this: a loop that re-decides the same wrong thing scores exactly as well as efficient progress. DIST-1 saw the mirror image (controller reaches Route 1 then wanders back; held only 4/6) and reported direction-locking at 41% of cycles. Done-when: runs report cycle-efficiency (decisions per map transition, and repeated-state/backtrack rate) alongside autonomy, so 'decided unaided' cannot be reported without 'and moved': the S0 baseline's 786.1 escalations-per-transition is the number this must be compared against.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T21:06:10.988705+00:00"}
